References: <K1BgMGVqKtA.K-l_wNrICfkJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/K1BgMGVqKtA/m/n8EexVg06vIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I would recommend doing the 2nd thing , treating the 2 years as independent sites or, as we say in previous postings to the email list "stack the years" (create a bigger data set with years as sites).
There will be very little dependence induced by having repeated measures on only 10 sites.
In any case you can bootstrap the SEs to fix that problem.

2. this is everyone's problem and there is no solution to it!   One idea is you could just embark on a massive data dredging exercise and fit all models and see what is the best. In a way this is kind of a model-based PCA. You're letting the data find the best linear combination of variables for explaining occupancy.

regards
andy

