Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

All,

I've posed the question on ECOLOG and have received some great responses, but I thought I'd post here (by recommendation of an ECOLOG subscriber):

I'm interested in knowing how to deal with home ranges exceeding plot size in the following scenario while maintaining the chance to make inference regarding abundance and PAO.

Scenario: Wildlife response to treatment polygons; oak-dominated ecosystem; occupancy modeling framework; focal species include bobcat, coyote, red fox, raccoon, gray fox, opossum, skunk and small mammals, with an emphasis on white-tailed deer and eastern wild turkey; four neighboring polygons each measuring roughly 2 sq km or less; total number of cameras available is 40; season is considered to be three months; extremely rough and steep terrain.

Additional info: I have recently found out that my advisor and collaborators on the project would like to use my data for a colleague's research, so I am expected to have a camera at each of my colleague's sampling points (which ends up with a camera density of 30 cameras/sq km). Will such camera density work? What argument do I have against such design?

Do I saturate or minimize the number of cameras?

B

