Thank you very much for your two cents; great thoughts on the second bullet point.

1. Each treatment polygon is <= 2 sq km. With respect to home ranges, it varies among species. Let's look at the white-tailed deer, the number varies, but I most recently read that males during the summer have a home range area of ~550 meters.
2. I do, but it will be in two week intervals. I plan to deploy 30 cameras in each polygon for a 14 day sampling period. I know with the "shuffle" approach one assumption that I have to make is that the occupancy status will not change during the season, but I don't believe it will. That is, I believe I have an equal probability of observing a raccoon at a site during the second week of June as I do during the third week of August.
3. To maintain constant coverage in the system, I would be able to deploy a maximum of 8 cameras per polygon. (I hope this answers your last question?)

4. My colleague isn't conducting a camera study. They are conducting a herbivory study using circular plots and exclosure fencing, hence that the collaborator would like cameras "extremely close" to their sites to be able to correlate results. My plan was to use a passive approach (non-baited sites), and yes, the plots are randomly plotted (i.e., on a random tree, in the middle of the forest :/ ).

