Hi Brent,

Thanks for the additional info.  So it sounds like 200 meter spacing, something in that neighborhood, maybe a total sampled area of about 5 square kilometers or so, and maybe 240 camera locations?

I don't know your study system, but given this spacing, you could probably treat stations as independent for any species with home ranges less than .04 square kilometers or so, and could probably consider correlated counts (and density)  for any species with a home range size between .05 and .5 km squared (assuming circular home ranges in both cases).   Moving from the small to the large end of the space use spectrum for the latter group of animals, ability to estimate density may suffer somewhat and ability to estimate resource use should improve somewhat (but I'd recommend taking a look at the design chapter in Andy's Spatial Capture-Recapture book).  So if many of the species you are interested in fall within those ranges, I'd think your design would be reasonable for inferring treatment effects.  I guess I'd recommend taking a look at Ramsey et al. 2015 in last month's JWM as well for some additional design guidance.

Just one though re. comment 2): That might be a concern for situations in which cameras are not spatially independent, but It's not clear to me that shuffling will violate closure if stations are independent.  If seasonal population change were an issue, then it would be easy enough to use some sort of date covariate for occupancy or whatever. And if not, I would think that the interpretation of psi would change to something like average probability of occupancy over the course of the 3 month survey (take with salt-grain...).

